WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.820
The prompt gamma ray flash can end quickly,

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:06.280
but an afterglow may remain. Rapid follow up

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.960
locates that fading counterpart and can connect it

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.280
to a host galaxy. A secure redshift provides

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.720
distance evidence. A nearly uniform distribution of burst

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.460
directions, by itself, cannot tell us how far

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:19.860
away the bursts are.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:22.690
In the lecture model for many long bursts,

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.670
a rapidly rotating massive star collapses and feeds

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:28.750
a central engine through a disk. Jets emerge

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:31.610
through the surrounding star. Many short bursts instead

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:34.730
come from compact mergers. A neutron star merger

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:36.970
with both gravitational waves and a gamma ray

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:40.590
signal supports that second pathway. Duration alone is

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.190
not a unique engine diagnosis.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.040
Now keep the source and its distance fixed,

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.580
but change the jet direction. A viewer inside

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.020
the beam can see a bright prompt burst.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:52.240
An off axis viewer can miss it even

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.960
though the explosion still occurs. Beaming makes the

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:57.860
detected sample smaller than the full event population;

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.040
it does not turn the engine off.
